<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Woodrow Wilson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1924">1924</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as President Wilson</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Woodrow Wilson was President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1913 to 1921, who led the country into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> in 1917. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a> of 1918 promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-determination">national self-determination</a> as a principle for the post-war settlement. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> discuss his role in the context of the empire's dissolution into national successor states in 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson.jpg" alt="Woodrow Wilson" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing, photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_Woodrow_Wilson_Harris_%26_Ewing_(3x4_cropped_b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
